# Synchronous vs. Asynchronous

We have been using callbacks for a while now. This lesson is about *why* JavaScript leans on them so heavily.

Suppose we need to do two **unrelated** tasks, A and B. Unrelated means we do not need A's result in order to do B, or the other way round. There are two styles:

- **Synchronous**: start A, wait for it to finish, then start B.
- **Asynchronous**: start A, and start B while A is still going.

**JavaScript runs code synchronously.** It is **single-threaded**, which means it can only do one thing at a time. And yet, with callbacks, we get many of the benefits of the asynchronous style.

## One Thing at a Time

That single-threaded fact has a consequence worth stating bluntly: **if JavaScript is stuck on something, nothing else can run.** No other code, no clicks, no typing, no scrolling. To the user, the browser has frozen.

Here is what that looks like. Suppose we want a message to change 5 seconds after a click. Written synchronously, we would need a line that genuinely takes 5 seconds:

```javascript
function synchronousPause(time) {
  const startTime = Date.now();
  while (Date.now() - startTime < time) {
    // do nothing, just burn time
  }
}

button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  timerContent.textContent = 'Timer started';
  synchronousPause(5000);                              // takes five seconds
  timerContent.textContent = 'Five seconds have passed';
});
```

(`synchronousPause` is not a real JavaScript function. It is something we wrote to simulate work that takes a long time. `Date.now()` gives the current time in milliseconds; we will meet it properly in the animation module.)

Do not write code like this. Here it is running, with the pause shortened to 1.5 seconds so the demo is bearable. There is a second button that counts its own clicks, so you can see what the freeze does to the rest of the page:

```html id=page1
<button id="start">Start blocking timer</button>
<button id="count">Click counter</button>
<p id="status">Not started.</p>
<p>Counter: <span id="counter">0</span></p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const startButton = document.querySelector('#start');
const countButton = document.querySelector('#count');
const status = document.querySelector('#status');
const counter = document.querySelector('#counter');

let clicks = 0;

countButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
  clicks++;
  counter.textContent = clicks;
});

function synchronousPause(time) {
  const startTime = Date.now();
  while (Date.now() - startTime < time) {
    // do nothing
  }
}

startButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
  status.textContent = 'Timer started';
  synchronousPause(1500);
  status.textContent = 'Time has passed';
});
```
```+id=page1
```

Click "Start blocking timer" and then immediately hammer the counter button. Nothing happens. The counter does not move, the page does not respond, and you may not even see `Timer started` appear, because the browser never got a chance to redraw. When the 1.5 seconds are up, everything unfreezes at once.

That is one line of JavaScript holding the entire page hostage.

## The Asynchronous Version

Here is the same behavior written properly:

```html id=page2
<button id="start">Start non-blocking timer</button>
<button id="count">Click counter</button>
<p id="status">Not started.</p>
<p>Counter: <span id="counter">0</span></p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const startButton = document.querySelector('#start');
const countButton = document.querySelector('#count');
const status = document.querySelector('#status');
const counter = document.querySelector('#counter');

let clicks = 0;

countButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
  clicks++;
  counter.textContent = clicks;
});

startButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
  status.textContent = 'Timer started';

  setTimeout(() => {
    status.textContent = 'Time has passed';
  }, 1500);

  console.log('this line runs immediately');
});
```
```+id=page2
```

Start the timer and hammer the counter again. It counts happily, the status message appears at once, and 1.5 seconds later it updates. Nothing was frozen.

The crucial difference: **the call to `setTimeout` itself takes no time at all.** It runs immediately, hands the browser a callback and a delay, and returns. The waiting is done by the browser, outside our code, and our call stack goes empty so everything else can carry on.

## The General Shape

Zoom out and every one of these functions has the same shape:

- We call some function (`addEventListener`, `setTimeout`, `setInterval`) and give it **when** to act and **what** to do, the callback.
- That function returns **immediately**. It may hand back some information about the task, like the timer ID `setTimeout` returns, or nothing at all, like `addEventListener`.
- Later, at the right moment, the browser puts our callback on the queue and it runs. It may be handed detail about what happened, like the event object.

The name says it: a callback is us saying "do not make me wait; call me back when it is time."

Anything that could take a long time in a browser is done this way. Timers are the familiar example. The important one, and the subject of this module, is fetching data over the network, which can take a second or more and must never freeze the page.

## Exercises

1. Make the cell log `first`, `third`, `second`, in that order, using a `setTimeout` with a `100` millisecond delay.

    ```{javascript}
    console.log('first');

    // Write your code here

    console.log('third');
    ```
    ```+solution
    console.log('first');

    setTimeout(() => {
      console.log('second');
    }, 100);

    console.log('third');
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(console.history().map(entry => entry.text).join(',')).should('equal', 'first,third').run('`first` and `third` should be logged straight away, in that order', 'The synchronous logs came first');
    setTimeout(() => {
      wrap(console.history().map(entry => entry.text).join(',')).should('equal', 'first,third,second').run('`second` should be logged last, from inside a `setTimeout` callback', 'The delayed log arrived last');
    }, 300);
    ```

2. Rewrite this blocking code so it does not freeze the page. The message should still change after `100` milliseconds.

    ```html id=page3
    <p id="status">idle</p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const status = document.querySelector('#status');

    function synchronousPause(time) {
      const startTime = Date.now();
      while (Date.now() - startTime < time) {
        // do nothing
      }
    }

    status.textContent = 'working';
    synchronousPause(100);
    status.textContent = 'done';
    ```
    ```+id=page3
    ```
    ```+solution
    const status = document.querySelector('#status');

    status.textContent = 'working';

    setTimeout(() => {
      status.textContent = 'done';
    }, 100);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(source.includes('while')).should('equal', false).run('Remove the blocking `while` loop entirely', 'No blocking loop left');
    wrap(source.includes('setTimeout')).should('equal', true).run('Use `setTimeout` to schedule the change instead', 'Used setTimeout');
    assert('#status').should('have.text', 'working').run('Straight after the cell runs, the message should read `working`', 'The first message shows immediately');
    setTimeout(() => {
      assert('#status').should('have.text', 'done').run('After the delay the message should read `done`', 'The delayed change happened');
    }, 300);
    ```

3. How long does the line `setTimeout(callback, 5000);` take to run?

    ```{mcq}
    A script reaches the line `setTimeout(callback, 5000);`. How long before the *next* line of that script runs?
    correct: Correct! `setTimeout` registers the timer and returns straight away. The five seconds are counted by the browser, not spent inside your code, which is exactly why the page stays responsive.
    wrong: Look back at "The Asynchronous Version" and what is said about how long the call itself takes.
    [x] Essentially no time at all; the next line runs immediately
    [ ] Five seconds
    [ ] Five seconds, but only if the callback is long
    [ ] It depends on how many other timers are running
    ```

